WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.799
Transfer is the ability to use learning outside the original lesson.

00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:07.833
Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles the practice activity.

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:12.205
Far transfer requires the learner to adapt the principle to a different context.

00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:16.576
Both matter, but real work often demands more than repeating the same example.

00:00:16.576 --> 00:00:19.265
Conditions are messy, information is incomplete, priorities compete,

00:00:19.265 --> 00:00:21.955
and the learner has to decide what applies.

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:25.654
Courses support transfer when they include realistic scenarios, meaningful decision points,

00:00:25.654 --> 00:00:26.663
and context variation.

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:29.016
Instead of showing only one perfect example,

00:00:29.016 --> 00:00:32.379
the learning experience can show several versions of the problem.

00:00:32.379 --> 00:00:34.732
What changes when the data is incomplete?

00:00:34.732 --> 00:00:36.750
What changes when time is limited?

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.776
What signal tells the learner which method to use?

00:00:39.776 --> 00:00:43.811
Variation helps learners recognize the underlying pattern rather than memorizing the surface

00:00:43.811 --> 00:00:45.492
details of a single exercise.

00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:49.190
Job aids, checklists, examples, and workflow references can also support transfer.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.880
They are not a sign that learning failed.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.588
They are part of good performance design, especially for tasks that are complex, infrequent,

00:00:56.588 --> 00:00:58.941
safety-sensitive, or easy to confuse under pressure.

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:02.976
The point is to help people use what they learned when the

00:01:02.976 --> 00:01:07.011
course is no longer open and the work has to be done

00:01:07.011 --> 00:01:08.356
in the real environment.

00:01:08.356 --> 00:01:10.037
A strong transfer question is,

00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:14.072
where will this learner first need the skill after the lesson ends?

00:01:14.072 --> 00:01:15.753
That question changes design decisions.

00:01:15.753 --> 00:01:19.115
It affects examples, practice conditions, language, timing, and support materials.

00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:23.487
If the first real use happens during a busy shift, a complex review,

00:01:23.487 --> 00:01:25.168
or a handoff between teams,

00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:29.203
the course should prepare learners for that context instead of only preparing

00:01:29.203 --> 00:01:31.220
them for a clean classroom version.
